<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-diamonds">
  <title>Data in the sky at night</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>"The proportions and relations of things are just as much facts as the things themselves."</p>
      <p>--- Dorothy L. Sayers</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The <c>diamonds</c> dataset from the R package <c>ggplot2</c> contains records for almost 54,000 round
      diamonds offered for sale.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Questions.</em> Do the data need cleaning? How are the variables distributed? Are there special features?
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Sources.</em> The data were scraped from the web in 2007 by Hadley Wickham and made available in
      <c>ggplot2</c>.
    </p>
    <p><em>Structure.</em> 53,940 round diamonds with information on price, dimensions, and quality.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-diamonds-data">
    <title>Data on diamonds for sale</title>

    <p>
      The first variables represent the four c's: carat, cut, colour, and clarity. Carat is weight; cut, colour,
      and clarity are ordered grades. Three measurements are recorded: length (<c>x</c>), width (<c>y</c>), and
      depth (<c>z</c>). Another variable called <c>depth</c> is actually percentage depth and is referred to here
      as <c>depthp</c>.
    </p>
    <p>
      A histogram of lengths suggests a tiny bar at zero and values extending beyond the central bulk. Boxplots are
      often better for highlighting outliers and axis stretch caused by extreme values.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-histlen">
      <image source="images/histLen-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Histogram of diamond lengths (the bar at zero has been circled in green).</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-boxlen">
      <image source="images/boxLen-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Boxplot of diamond lengths (outliers coloured red).</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The lowest values reveal eight zero-length diamonds. Zero values also appear for width and depth. A practical
      cleaning step is to remove these clearly erroneous records when source correction is not available.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-histlen2">
      <image source="images/histLen2-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Histogram of diamond lengths (without the zero-length diamonds).</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      With cleaner data and finer binwidth, the distribution suggests mixtures of groups. Similar patterns occur for
      width and depth, with related structure in carat values.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-histcar">
      <image source="images/histCar-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Histogram of diamond carats.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      One-sided peaks suggest many more diamonds at rounded carat values than just below them. This likely reflects
      market preferences and measurement granularity.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-histprice">
      <image source="images/histPrice-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Histogram of diamond prices.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The price histogram also reveals an unexpected gap around $1500: no values between $1455 and $1545. This is
      likely a scraping artifact.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-diamonds-outliers">
    <title>Checking for outliers and unusual values</title>

    <p>
      There are 20 diamonds with zero values for length, width, or depth, clearly erroneous. After removing these,
      three extreme high outliers remain for width and depth.
    </p>
    <p>
      A scatterplot matrix of cleaned dimensions and cube-root carat shows high correlations with visible
      two-dimensional outliers.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-scatxyz">
      <image source="images/scatxyz-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          A scatterplot matrix of length, width, depth, and carats to the power one third for the dataset after
          removing the zero cases and three extreme high outliers.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Additional checks with carat, depth percentage, table, and price expose further unusual values and support
      iterative cleaning.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-scatfour">
      <image source="images/scatFour-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          A scatterplot matrix of carat, percentage depth, table, and price for the dataset after removing the zero
          cases and four extreme outliers.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ecdfdot">
      <image source="images/ecdfdot-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          A vertically jittered dotplot of diamond table, excluding 12 outlying values, showing heaping at integer
          values.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Reported percentage depth can be compared to percentage depth recalculated from length, width, and depth.
      Cases where differences exceed 1 indicate possible inconsistencies requiring deeper inspection.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-bothdep">
      <image source="images/bothDep-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          A scatterplot of reported percentage depth (<c>depthp</c>) and percentage depth calculated from the data
          (after removing the zero cases and three extreme outliers). Cases where the difference is bigger than 1
          have been coloured red and drawn last.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-diamonds-intriguing">
    <title>Intriguing features</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Scatterplots of price appear bounded at the top. An enlarged price-carat plot with a guideline at $19,000
        highlights this ceiling effect.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-scatprice">
        <image source="images/scatPrice-1.png"/>
        <caption>
          <p>A scatterplot of price against carat with a red line at $19000, just above the maximum reported price.</p>
        </caption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        This boundary may reflect how the dataset was collected or the exclusion of very high-value stones from the
        source listings. Such truncation can bias models.
      </p>
      <p>
        Associations of carat with categorical quality variables show systematic shifts in median carat by clarity,
        colour, and cut.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-claritycarat">
        <image source="images/clarityCarat-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Boxplots of carat by clarity and by colour.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-cutcarat">
        <image source="images/cutCarat-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Boxplots of carat by cut.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <em>Answers.</em> The data have cleaning issues (zero values, outliers, and missing price ranges).
        One-sided peaks in carat are a notable feature, and the apparent upper price limit affects inference.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Further questions.</em> What are the distributions of colour, cut, and clarity? What influence do colour
        and cut have on price?
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-diamonds-takeaways">
      <title>Graphical takeaways</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>For large datasets, very narrow histogram binwidths can reveal hidden structure.</p></li>
        <li><p>Single-dimension outliers can distort higher-dimensional displays as well.</p></li>
        <li><p>Surprising plot shapes often indicate real structure worth investigating.</p></li>
        <li><p>Parallel boxplots are a quick and informative way to compare groups.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
